**Theorem 4.6 (birth-trim estimates for a fixed ordered monomorphism injection).** Let $A$ and $B$ be arbitrary finite positive-length interval families over an arbitrary field, with real births and finite or infinite deaths. Fix increasing-birth enumerations in each death fiber and an occurrence injection $j:A\hookrightarrow B$ preserving death and fiber ordinal, with $b_{j(a)}\le b_a$. Let $m:I_A\to I_B$ be an actual componentwise injective natural map. For every $\eta\ge0$ satisfying $\operatorname{im}(I_B(t\to t+\eta))\subseteq\operatorname{im}m_{t+\eta}$ at every real $t$, one has $b_a\le b_{j(a)}+\eta$. Every $B$ occurrence with $b+\eta<d$ is in the range of this same fixed $j$, and every essential occurrence is in its range. If $\eta=0$, $j$ is surjective and paired births agree.

**Proof.** Retain $B$ occurrences with $b+\eta<d$ and replace their births by $b+\eta$. Zero extension gives an injective natural trim inclusion whose range at $t$ equals the actual image from $t-\eta$ to $t$. The range condition lifts it through $m_t$ by the inverse of the domain-to-range equivalence. Cancelling $m$ proves naturality. The ordered monomorphism injection from this trim to $A$ sends the first $k+1$ occurrences below any long $B$ occurrence into $A$ occurrences with birth at most that occurrence's birth plus $\eta$. This forces the fixed ordinal-$k$ occurrence to exist and satisfy the bound. Short matched occurrences satisfy the bound by positivity and equal death. Essentials remain in the trim; zero trimming retains every positive occurrence.

